Negative control for world-adopt.spec.ts (T4.3, AC2.5 — design §3.1 ruling 2 /
§7.1, the ADOPT branch).

`useModulePort` reaches the cell through the scope registry, which caches by
scope key — so the world's port IS the cell the page is rendering. This mutant
restores the unconditional `dispose()` at the head of `boot`, which is what the
composable did before T4.3: booting the scope already on screen calls `destroy()`
on the page's own cell and mints a second port over the wreckage. In the app that
is a track tearing the rendered page down the moment it is armed.

The mutant is deliberately surgical — it removes only the adopt guard, leaving
`booted` assigned and unread. `pnpm --filter @upmind-automation/labs-nuxt test`
transpiles rather than typechecks, so the mutated file runs; the dead write is
the tell that the guard is the one thing that went.

**This mutant is the ONLY net for the adopt branch.** Per `tasks.md` K12 the
Playwright bridge lane never executes it: one world is minted per page load and
each driven scenario `goto`s a fresh page, so the first `boot` always runs with
`port === undefined` and `dispose()` is a no-op. The lane stays green whether the
adopt rule is right or wrong.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: world-adopt.spec.ts — the adopt cases (re-booting the same key+scope
neither fires the cell's `destroy` nor mints a second port; the live port is the
one still answering `fire`/`expectMeta`).

